 "title": "Сладкий сон  (A Sweet Dream)",
 "level": "A1 — Duolingo Section 2",
 "labels": {
  "barinov": "Шеф",
  "max": "Макс"
 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Max has fallen asleep in the dining room and is having a lovely dream about the chef. When Barinov shakes him awake, Max is still half in the dream and says things he really shouldn't. Here's the Russian first.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Ты молодец, Витька.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Витюшенька, мой огузок.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Ты что, совсем с ума сошёл?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Ты что здесь спишь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Шеф, простите меня, пожалуйста.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я прилёг на пять минут.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "На кухне спать нельзя!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я всю ночь не мог уснуть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Почему ты ночью не спал?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Наверное, это акклиматизация.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Какая ещё акклиматизация?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я ещё не привык к Москве.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Быстро вставай и мой посуду!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Уже встаю, шеф.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Now let's see what Max says in his sleep.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Ты молодец, Витька.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Good job, Vitka.' The phrase ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Ты молодец,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to 'you're a fine fellow', the everyday praise. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Витька.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is an overfamiliar short form of the name Viktor, and Max uses the familiar 'you' with his boss in his sleep.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Витюшенька, мой огузок.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Vityushenka, my Oguzok.' The Russian term for this doting pet form of Viktor is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Витюшенька,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " built from extra syllables for tenderness. Max then flips the chef's nickname for him back onto the chef by calling him ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "мой огузок.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Ты что, совсем с ума сошёл?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Have you completely lost your mind?' The phrase ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "с ума сошёл?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally translates to 'came down off the mind'. Starting with ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Ты что,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to 'what's wrong with you?'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Ты что здесь спишь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Why are you sleeping here?' Here, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Ты что,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " does the job of 'why', as in everyday speech. The term for 'you sleep' is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "спишь?",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Шеф, простите меня, пожалуйста.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Chef, please forgive me.' Awake, Max switches to the polite 'you'. The Russian term for 'forgive' is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "простите",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " built from the polite extra letters at the end.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я прилёг на пять минут.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I lay down for five minutes.' The added part turns the translation for 'lay down' into ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "прилёг",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " which literally translates to 'lay down for a bit'. And ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "на пять минут.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to 'for five minutes'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "На кухне спать нельзя!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You cannot sleep in the kitchen!' The expression for 'it's not allowed' is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "нельзя!",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " It needs no person doing the action. The action word ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "спать",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to the plain 'to sleep'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я всю ночь не мог уснуть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I could not sleep all night.' The phrase for 'all night' is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "всю ночь",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " which needs no extra little word for 'during'. The phrase ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "не мог уснуть.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to 'could not fall asleep', built from the man's past form of 'can'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Почему ты ночью не спал?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Why didn't you sleep at night?' The translation for 'at night' is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "ночью",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " built from 'night' with an extra sound at the end. Other times of day work the same way, taking a similar extra sound to mean 'in the morning' or 'in the evening'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Наверное, это акклиматизация.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Probably it is acclimatization.' Max gives the same long borrowed word as before for 'acclimatization', ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "акклиматизация.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " And the word for 'probably' is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Наверное,",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Какая ещё акклиматизация?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What acclimatization?' The phrase ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Какая ещё",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " repeats the word back with an edge, which translates to 'what acclimatization, exactly?'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я ещё не привык к Москве.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I am not used to Moscow yet.' The phrase that translates to 'got used to' is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "привык к",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " and the little word linking to the city changes its spelling, making it ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "к Москве.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " The phrase that translates to 'not yet' is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "ещё не",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Быстро вставай и мой посуду!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Quickly get up and wash dishes!' The Russian expression for telling someone to 'wash' here is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "мой",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " and it is spelled the same as the translation for 'my'. The term for 'dishes' is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "посуду!",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " which translates to one singular thing in Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Уже встаю, шеф.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Already getting up, Chef.' The phrase ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Уже встаю,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to 'already I get up'. The plain action word stands in for 'I am getting up'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
